<section id="migration"><span class="compat-anchor" id="release-details-title" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="compat-anchor" id="release-path-title" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<h2>0.72.0 upgrades with no configuration change</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Keep the binaries together.</strong> Upgrade <code>dekopon-brokerd</code>, <code>dekopon-gatewayd</code> and any console together. Preserve the previous binaries, configuration and provider lock. Stop the gateway before the broker; start the broker first and verify the real gateway afterward.</li>
<li><strong>Check a shell-sourced file first.</strong> The <code>--env-file</code> loader accepts a leading <code>export</code> and quoted values but expands nothing, so a value that relied on <code>$</code>, escapes or command substitution must become literal. <code>check</code> refuses the option; source the file in a shell for <code>check --probe</code>. Run both configuration checks as the owning service users.</li>
<li><strong>Remove the new options before rolling back.</strong> A 0.73.0 daemon rejects <code>--env-file</code>: remove it from the launch arguments and load the secrets another way first. A 0.72.0 gateway also rejects the two new <code>sessions.wakes</code> keys and refuses a wakes file that holds a PR subscription or a channel-owned wake; deleting that file loses every pending wake.</li>
</ol>
<p>Older installations may also cross these changes: OTLP uses HTTP/protobuf only, so replace <code>telemetry.transport: grpc</code>; journal configurations require <code>sessions.assets.path</code>; and journal v3 resets older-format records at startup instead of migrating them. Earlier ones also need the <code>dekopond</code> rename and <code>dekopon.dev/gatewayd/v1alpha1</code> configuration API. Brokers accept the <code>provider@0.4.0</code> stream contract and refuse older <code>provider@0.3.0</code> components. By default, managed sets load from a compatible compiled cache: sync populates it, and <code>dekopon-brokerd provider precompile</code> fills or repairs it after an engine change.</p>
<p>The <a href="{{ source }}/docs/upgrading.md">release-pinned upgrade guide ↗</a> gives each breaking change and its rollback limits. Use the <a href="/deploy/mac/">Mac guide</a> or <a href="/deploy/kubernetes/#upgrade">Kubernetes checklist</a> for the process and storage layout.</p>
</section>

<section id="limits"><span class="compat-anchor" id="release-scope-title" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<h2>A channel wake gets only the channel's grants</h2>
<p>A PR re-review, digest or other wake created in a mapped channel runs as that space alone, so a participant's wider personal grant doesn't carry over. Grant the space its reads and any optional <code>COMMENT</code> permission. A subscription ends at close, merge or expiry (14 days by default), and a push never extends it. Pending wakes share one file written without fsync; restart the gateway at the wrong moment and a due wake is gone.</p>
<p><code>--env-file</code> changes how a daemon reads its secrets, not where they live: the file holds literal values on disk, and anything running as that daemon's user can read it. Keep a GitHub PAT in the broker's credential file, because the broker reads no credential from its environment.</p>
<p>Detached jobs remain opt-in through <code>limits.jobTimeoutMs</code>. Provider child scripts use <code>dekopon:spawn</code> under the caller's shared limits. Neither feature gives a provider access to the Mac's host shell; the optional VM runner is a separate Linux/KVM service.</p>
<p>Dekopon is pre-1.0. Cedar checks declared access, not intent. A permitted endpoint receives its credential, and telemetry retention remains your responsibility. Journal replay restores conversation data; it does not resume a paid effect or undo one that already ran.</p>
</section>

